{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c415","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c4ab-e99a-71a1-b01c-d4e1f789add0"},"sequence":101,"recorded_at":1780531400000197,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"e9eaa535-0e9f-444e-ac97-d216243d9df7","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"e9eaa535-0e9f-444e-ac97-d216243d9df7","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"e9eaa535-0e9f-444e-ac97-d216243d9df7"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0c4ac-3735-7780-bc4f-1b24392e4645"},"task_id":"01a0c4ac-3735-7780-bc4f-1b24392e4645","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0c4ac-3735-7780-bc4f-1b24392e4645","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-35urbp/TASK-260830-35urbp_rework-rev4.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-35urbp_rework-rev3.md and every earlier precondition. Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-35urbp, the FIRST of three leaves of STORY-260830-2t4g7i. Revision 3 was reviewed by RUN-260921-2369f7 (claude-opus-5 max) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED with **no P1**: three P2 and five P3. Read TASK-260830-35urbp_review-verdict-rev3.md first. The reviewer states that **every rev2 finding — P2-A, P3-A..E and the three observations — is closed with driven evidence and stays closed**. Keep all of it. Production is correct at every point below except the two spec-text divergences in items 1 and 2; the rest is entry-level pinning and honest prose.\n3|Trunk has NOT moved (base is still 799c338). Your working tree is the rev3 candidate; build revision 4 on it. Do not refresh, do not rebase.\n4|\n5|**FIRST, MY ERROR, BECAUSE IT EXPLAINS ITEM 1.** The producer brief I wrote named §4.2 and §4.C-4.E as your authority. That list was incomplete: **§3.2 governs exactly this leaf's deliverable** — it fixes the socket path and the runtime root — and because I did not name it, rounds 1 through 3 never checked against it and your suite grew tests that protect the divergence. AGENTS.md makes the whole pinned specification the authority for product behaviour, so §3.2 was always binding. Treat it as authority from here.\n6|\n7|1. **P2-A — the derived path is not the path the specification mandates.** Pinned `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md` §3.2, lines 806-813: \"On macOS the tmux backend MUST use a dedicated AX server selected by `tmux -S <runtime>/tmux/ax.sock`. The `<runtime>/tmux` parent MUST be AX-created, owned by the current user, mode 0700, and verified component-by-component without following a symlink.\" Today `RuntimeDirName = \"ax-tmux\"` and `SocketName = \"ax-tmux.sock\"` derive `<root>/ax-tmux/ax-tmux.sock`; the spec says `<root>/tmux/ax.sock`. Worse, `TestSocketPathDerivesOnlyFromRuntimeDir` pins the literal `\"ax-tmux.sock\"`, so the suite defends the divergence instead of detecting it. Two things must change together: the literals become `tmux` and `ax.sock`, asserted in that test as the spec tokens plus the joined form `<root>/tmux/ax.sock`; and `Request.Root` stops being documented as \"the AX state root\" and becomes the **Runtime IPC root** of the §3.2 table (macOS: per-user temporary directory; Linux/WSL2: `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/ax`). The landed `internal/localstore` already resolves it as `PathRuntime` (paths.go:332-366) — consume that, do not derive your own; \"the socket is runtime IPC, never durable identity\" (SPEC 810) is the reason, and it is also why the post-reboot absence case in item 2 is the normal cold state rather than an edge. Add a §3.2 row (path + custody clause) to TRACEABILITY and the conformance matrix, and update README and `doc.go`. I am not authorising a bound here: implement the spec's path.\n8|2. **P2-B — a background caller with an absent runtime directory gets a custody code where the spec mandates `capability_unavailable`.** §4.2 lines 1419-1423 is explicit: if neither an authenticated broker nor an attested server exists, the entry \"MUST return `capability_unavailable` with typed realm/readiness details and MUST NOT fall back to direct server creation.\" Today `acquireBackground` calls `VerifyRuntimeDir` first and returns its refusal verbatim, so an absent runtime directory yields `tmux_unsafe_runtime_dir at runtime containment` and the broker is never probed. With the Runtime IPC root being a per-user temporary directory, that is the ordinary post-reboot state of every background restore worker. Everything the rev2 review verified must stay — verify-never-create on the authorizing path, no durable write, no broker probe past a custody refusal. **Only the classification of ABSENCE changes**: a missing leaf is a readiness fact (there is nothing to contact) and must surface as the typed `capability_unavailable` with the request's realm/readiness details; a leaf that EXISTS but fails mode, ownership, kind or symlink custody is unsafe per SPEC 810-812 and keeps its custody code. This requires the verify side to distinguish `ENOENT` from `ENOTDIR`/`ELOOP`/other, which is item 6 — do them as one change. Update `TestAcquireBackgroundVerifiesWithoutCreating` to assert `capability_unavailable` plus typed details plus no directory plus zero spawns, keep the existing custody-refusal siblings, ship a narrowing row for the absence arm, and fix README and rows 13/52, which currently claim behaviour the probes disprove.\n9|3. **P2-C — the foreground \"running server → attach-or-refuse, never spawn\" rule is pinned at the entry only for servers that carry admission data.** Every committed `Running: true` test supplies both non-empty `Admitted.Capabilities` and a non-empty `RawGeneration`. The realistic post-spawn state — server running, nothing reconciled yet, `RealmAdmission{}` — is driven only at the helper, never through `Acquire` (rejection pattern b). Two entry narrowings survived both passes: treating a running server with zero admission rows as absent, and the same for a running server whose admission carries no generation; under either, `Acquire` spawns a second `new-session` into a live server. Production refuses correctly today (`tmux_readiness_not_authorizing at server attestation`, and `server generation`, with zero spawns). Commit both entry-level tests and ship `N-running-empty-admission` and `N-running-empty-generation` as those narrowings, named under rows 41/42.\n10|4. **P3-A — the TMUX-env collision member is measured on a shape tmux never produces.** tmux sets `TMUX=<socket>,<pid>,<index>`, and your hostile fixture uses exactly that encoding, but the collision rows feed the bare derived path into `TMUXEnv`. A `TMUX` value naming the derived socket in the real encoding walks past `checkAmbientDisjoint` and `Acquire` spawns. Either compare the socket component (everything before the first comma) for that member, with a test in the real encoding, or state the bound under B16 and reword row 23 so it stops claiming every member.\n11|5. **P3-C — root custody is neither gated nor bounded.** A world-writable (0777) root admits the leaf on `EnsureRuntimeDir`, `VerifyRuntimeDir` and the background broker path. SPEC 810-812 requires rejecting a socket path, parent or ancestor whose ownership or permissions are unsafe, before bind or connect. B11 covers symlinked intermediates only. Likewise \"AX-created\" (SPEC 808) is not distinguishable after the fact: any same-user 0700 directory under an admitted name is accepted. State both as explicit bounds **naming the owner** (the `localstore` layout owner, the lifecycle bind step in TASK-260830-1c28dz, or this leaf), or gate the root's mode and ownership on the pinned root handle. Do not leave it unstated a fourth time.\n12|6. **P3-B — absence and a failure to read are the same fact on the verify side.** `verifyRuntimeDir` maps `ENOENT`, `ENOTDIR`/`ELOOP` and a missing root all to `runtime containment`, while the commit side maps a missing root to `tmux_invalid_arguments at runtime root`. Give absence its own detail (item 2 consumes it) and align the missing-root mapping across the two entries so rows 10/13 name the same code.\n13|7. **P3-D and P3-E — prose and two unpinned arms.** `socket.go` lines 28-31 still say both environment names \"are validated by the landed environment-name grammar at the observation boundary, so a misspelled variable cannot smuggle an ambient value past the non-use proof\"; that arm was deleted in rev2, no `environ`/`secprim` import remains, and nothing is validated — fix the comment and drop `ObserveAmbient`'s now-always-nil `error` return, or state what the return is for. Then add the two missing rows or report them as bounds: a permission failure at `Mkdirat` treated as `EEXIST` survives because no committed test stages a read-only root (the `runtime commit` detail is unmeasured), and a whitespace-only override survives because the override gate is pinned on five hostile literals only. Neither admits an unsafe state, so a bound is acceptable — but pick one and say it.\n14|\n15|PROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. Run your own package tests, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...` (keep it green), and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once, reading the commands from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` in the candidate config. Update the results with a finding-by-finding table (finding -> change -> the test that fails without it -> evidence path), update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive (a real gzip archive, MANIFEST with no self-entry, one raw log per plant with its subprocess exit, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, zero `__pycache__`), re-complete the checklist, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-35urbp --role developer` so revision 4 is constructed, and exit promptly. Report the ratio you MEASURE. Hand off no later than 2.5 hours in. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The same claude-opus-5 max reviewer contract applies to revision 4.\n16|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n17|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n18|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n19|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n20|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n21|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n22|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n23|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.","final_result":true}}}
